      "title": "The seven dirty words",
      "why_it_works": "Treats a language taboo as a constitutional question. rigidify: applies the rules of 'obscene' literally until the category collapses. The bit is jurisprudence \u2014 the courtroom is the stage, the audience is the jury.",
      "theory_links": [
        "rigidify",
        "literalise",
        "status_reversal"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Euphemism execution (soft language)",
      "why_it_works": "Takes a soft phrase ('passed away', 'persons of substance') and holds it to its literal meaning until the euphemism confesses. The list accelerates like a sermon; the key word lands like a gavel. The room laughs at the recognition of the lie they've been telling.",
      "theory_links": [
        "rigidify",
        "literalise"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The list as sermon form",
      "why_it_works": "Lists are rhetorical acceleration devices \u2014 the room is carried forward by the rhythm, the key word is the amen. The list is not a collection of jokes; it's a rhetorical structure that makes the final word inevitable and devastating.",
      "theory_links": [
        "escalation",
        "repetition_variation"
      ]
    }
